WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.084
How long do requests to / take?

2
00:00:05.084 --> 00:00:07.468
An average hides the slow ones.

3
00:00:07.468 --> 00:00:11.083
So teams ask for a percentile, such as the p95.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:19.413
A p95 of 200 milliseconds means 95% of requests took 200 milliseconds or less.

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:23.967
In PromQL, the p95 for hello-devops is this one line.

6
00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:25.193
It looks scary.

7
00:00:25.193 --> 00:00:27.483
Read it from the inside out.

8
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.076
There are five steps.

9
00:00:29.076 --> 00:00:31.959
Each one takes the output of the one before.

10
00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:34.287
Latency lives in a histogram.

11
00:00:34.287 --> 00:00:39.113
Each bucket is a counter, and its le label is a limit, in seconds.

12
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:42.660
The selector picks every bucket series for path /.

13
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.853
One series per bucket, so one column per series.

14
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:52.324
Every 15 seconds, each scrape adds one row: every counter's total since the process started.

15
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:55.255
Step two, the range in square brackets.

16
00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:58.604
[5m] keeps every sample from the last 5 minutes.

17
00:00:58.604 --> 00:01:02.303
At a 15 second scrape, that is 20 samples per series.

18
00:01:02.533 --> 00:01:05.753
Make the range too short, and it fails quietly.

19
00:01:05.753 --> 00:01:09.657
[10s] holds only one sample, and rate needs at least two.

20
00:01:09.657 --> 00:01:12.397
The series just drops out of the result.

21
00:01:12.397 --> 00:01:13.013
No error.

22
00:01:13.233 --> 00:01:15.743
So use at least 4 times the scrape interval.

23
00:01:15.743 --> 00:01:18.537
Then a failed scrape still leaves enough samples.

24
00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:19.825
Step three, rate.

25
00:01:19.825 --> 00:01:26.545
Roughly, it subtracts the first sample in the window from the last, and divides by the seconds between them.

26
00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:29.032
Each column collapses to one number.

27
00:01:29.032 --> 00:01:33.500
Each bucket now reads: requests per second that took this long or less.

28
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.060
Say 100 requests a second arrive.

29
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:43.271
80 of them finished within 0.01 seconds, and 97 within 0.025.

30
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.069
Step four, sum by le.

31
00:01:45.069 --> 00:01:51.193
It adds up paths and instances, bucket by bucket, and keeps le, the bucket limits.

32
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:56.505
Drop le, and the result is empty, because the last step needs those limits.

33
00:01:56.733 --> 00:02:01.022
This is also why you never average percentiles from several servers.

34
00:02:01.022 --> 00:02:04.555
Sum the buckets first, then compute the percentile once.

35
00:02:05.367 --> 00:02:09.004
Step five, histogram_quantile, with 0.95.

36
00:02:09.004 --> 00:02:11.311
Draw each bucket as a bar.

37
00:02:11.533 --> 00:02:13.954
95% of 100 is 95.

38
00:02:13.954 --> 00:02:16.091
Draw that line.

39
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.989
The 0.01 bucket holds 80, below the line.

40
00:02:20.989 --> 00:02:25.107
The 0.025 bucket holds 97, above it.

41
00:02:25.107 --> 00:02:32.427
So the 95th request is somewhere between 0.01 and 0.025 seconds.

42
00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:36.085
That bucket holds 17 requests, 97 minus 80.

43
00:02:36.085 --> 00:02:39.537
Prometheus does not know their exact times.

44
00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:42.931
So it assumes they are spread evenly across the bucket.

45
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:46.980
The 95th request is the 15th of those 17.

46
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:56.216
So the answer is 0.01, plus 15 seventeenths of the bucket's 0.015 second width.

47
00:02:56.216 --> 00:02:58.499
About 0.023 seconds.

48
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.623
So the answer is always an estimate.

49
00:03:01.623 --> 00:03:03.723
Both of these happened in the lab.

50
00:03:03.933 --> 00:03:09.521
Prometheus's own query API answered in a few milliseconds.

51
00:03:09.521 --> 00:03:17.131
But its first bucket is 0 to 0.1 seconds, so the p95 came out at exactly 0.095.

52
00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:27.217
During a Redis outage, hello-devops's p95 read 6.12 seconds: somewhere in the 5 to 7.5 second bucket.

53
00:03:27.433 --> 00:03:31.003
So choose bucket limits around the latencies you care about.

54
00:03:31.833 --> 00:03:40.203
A p95 query, inside out: select the buckets, take a range, rate, sum by le, then estimate the quantile.
